#X obj 230 93 cnv 1 535 340 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -257983 -1 0;
#X text 240 98 Note: default PWM freq is 50Hz;
#X text 240 123 *Servo Control*;
#X text 240 148 Many digital servos run on a 20ms pulse width (50Hz
update frequency) based duty cycle \, and do not utilize the entire
pulse width for their control;
#X text 240 203 Typically \, 2.5% of the 20ms pulse width (0.5ms) represents
-90° offset \, and 12.5% of the 20ms pulse width (2.5ms) represents
+90° offset;
#X text 240 258 Be aware that driving some 180° servos too far past
their -90°/+90° operational range can cause a little plastic limiter
pin to break off and get stuck inside of the servo's gearing \, which
could potentially cause the servo to become jammed and no longer function;
#X text 240 343 Continuous servos operate in much the same fashion
as 180° servos \, but instead of the 2.5%/12.5% pulse width controlling
a -90°/+90° offset it controls a -1x/+1x speed multiplier \, with 0x
being parked/no-movement and -1x/+1x being maximum speed in either
direction.;
#X text 50 481 180° servo example;
